1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
This is the story of a schoolteacher
in Virginia.

2
00:00:03,260 --> 00:00:07,260
Forty years old, married,
with an ordinary, stable life.

3
00:00:07,906 --> 00:00:11,006
Then, in 2000, something in him changed.

4
00:00:11,626 --> 00:00:16,346
He developed sexual urges towards
children, urges he had never had before.

5
00:00:16,726 --> 00:00:18,866
And he began to act on them.

6
00:00:19,355 --> 00:00:22,285
He was convicted
and sent to a treatment programme.

7
00:00:22,515 --> 00:00:25,775
Even there, he couldn't stop,
and he was thrown out.

8
00:00:26,429 --> 00:00:28,749
The night before his sentencing,
he went to hospital

9
00:00:28,979 --> 00:00:31,129
with a headache that wouldn't go away.

10
00:00:31,829 --> 00:00:34,889
He told the doctors he knew
what he was doing was wrong.

11
00:00:35,269 --> 00:00:37,049
He just couldn't stop himself.

12
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:40,920
A brain scan found a tumour
the size of an egg,

13
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:44,640
pressing on a part of the brain
that helps us hold back our impulses.

14
00:00:45,505 --> 00:00:47,185
Surgeons removed it.

15
00:00:47,445 --> 00:00:49,045
The urges went away.

16
00:00:49,525 --> 00:00:51,985
He finished the programme
and went back home.

17
00:00:52,581 --> 00:00:55,301
Less than a year later,
the headaches came back.

18
00:00:55,621 --> 00:00:57,121
So did the urges.

19
00:00:57,927 --> 00:00:59,627
The tumour had grown back.

20
00:01:00,087 --> 00:01:03,427
Surgeons removed it again,
and again, the urges disappeared.

21
00:01:06,167 --> 00:01:07,747
So here's the question.

22
00:01:08,287 --> 00:01:10,447
Was he responsible for what he did?

23
00:01:11,927 --> 00:01:14,347
Most people would say: not really.

24
00:01:14,687 --> 00:01:16,207
Something physical in his brain

25
00:01:16,437 --> 00:01:19,687
changed what he wanted,
and he never chose that tumour.

26
00:01:20,175 --> 00:01:22,275
But what if there was no tumour?

27
00:01:22,455 --> 00:01:26,555
What if every choice you've ever made
was shaped by things you never chose?

28
00:01:27,313 --> 00:01:30,953
Your genes. Your childhood.
The people around you.

29
00:01:31,253 --> 00:01:33,633
The state of your brain
at this very second.

30
00:01:34,259 --> 00:01:37,499
His case only looks special
because we can see the cause.

31
00:01:38,179 --> 00:01:39,819
It's right there, on a scan.

32
00:01:40,544 --> 00:01:44,284
For the rest of us, the causes
are spread out, tangled and invisible.

33
00:01:44,844 --> 00:01:47,584
But invisible doesn't mean
they aren't there.

34
00:01:48,130 --> 00:01:50,210
So let's ask a stranger question.

35
00:01:50,290 --> 00:01:54,750
Not whether we have free will, but what
it would actually mean if we didn't.

36
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:00,560
For a while, it looked like science
had already settled it.

37
00:02:01,055 --> 00:02:03,395
In 1983, a researcher called

38
00:02:03,625 --> 00:02:06,975
Benjamin Libet asked people
to do something very simple:

39
00:02:07,335 --> 00:02:10,135
flick their wrist,
whenever they felt like it.

40
00:02:10,644 --> 00:02:14,804
He measured their brain activity,
and had them watch a dot circling a clock

41
00:02:15,034 --> 00:02:18,084
and note the exact moment
they decided to move.

42
00:02:18,761 --> 00:02:20,341
The brain went first.

43
00:02:21,121 --> 00:02:22,981
A slow build-up of electrical activity

44
00:02:23,211 --> 00:02:25,961
began about half a second
before the movement.

45
00:02:26,646 --> 00:02:29,246
The feeling of wanting to move came later,

46
00:02:29,346 --> 00:02:32,306
only about a fifth of a second
before the movement.

47
00:02:32,993 --> 00:02:36,473
In other words, the brain seemed
to get going a third of a second

48
00:02:36,553 --> 00:02:39,513
before the person was even aware
of wanting to move.

49
00:02:40,129 --> 00:02:42,429
Later experiments pushed it further.

50
00:02:43,389 --> 00:02:46,449
In 2008, researchers used brain scans

51
00:02:46,679 --> 00:02:49,489
to predict which of two buttons
people would press,

52
00:02:49,709 --> 00:02:52,669
up to seven seconds before
they were aware of choosing.

53
00:02:53,304 --> 00:02:56,204
It looked as if consciousness
wasn't the boss at all.

54
00:02:56,564 --> 00:03:00,944
More like a press office, announcing
decisions that had already been made.

55
00:03:01,573 --> 00:03:04,273
But look closer,
and it gets less dramatic.

56
00:03:05,033 --> 00:03:09,253
That 2008 prediction was right
about sixty per cent of the time.

57
00:03:09,933 --> 00:03:11,733
A coin toss gets fifty.

58
00:03:12,203 --> 00:03:17,043
And in 2012, another team offered a
different explanation for Libet's signal.

59
00:03:17,633 --> 00:03:19,493
The brain is never quiet.

60
00:03:20,193 --> 00:03:23,033
Its activity drifts up and down
all the time.

61
00:03:23,513 --> 00:03:26,953
When it happens to drift over a threshold,
a movement gets triggered.

62
00:03:27,331 --> 00:03:30,251
So if you only look at the moments
just before a movement,

63
00:03:30,331 --> 00:03:31,951
you will always see a rise.

64
00:03:32,651 --> 00:03:35,171
Not because the decision came early,

65
00:03:35,251 --> 00:03:38,071
but because that's what
crossing a line looks like.

66
00:03:38,544 --> 00:03:42,724
So the experiments that were supposed
to disprove free will don't quite do that.

67
00:03:45,504 --> 00:03:47,644
But here's the uncomfortable part.

68
00:03:48,344 --> 00:03:51,184
The real problem never depended
on those experiments.

69
00:03:51,981 --> 00:03:54,001
Think of any decision you made today.

70
00:03:54,321 --> 00:03:56,481
What to eat. What to say.

71
00:03:56,861 --> 00:03:58,741
Whether to keep watching this video.

72
00:03:59,077 --> 00:04:04,237
That decision had causes.
Your mood. Your hunger. Your habits.

73
00:04:04,317 --> 00:04:07,537
The way your neurons
happened to be wired this morning.

74
00:04:07,817 --> 00:04:10,637
And each of those causes
had causes of its own,

75
00:04:10,797 --> 00:04:12,937
going back to your parents, your genes,

76
00:04:13,037 --> 00:04:15,517
and things that happened
long before you were born.

77
00:04:16,127 --> 00:04:19,587
So where in that chain is the part of you
that's free from all of it?

78
00:04:19,867 --> 00:04:22,767
The part that steps in from outside
and decides?

79
00:04:23,606 --> 00:04:25,566
Maybe the chain isn't complete.

80
00:04:26,246 --> 00:04:29,626
Maybe somewhere, deep in physics,
there's genuine randomness.

81
00:04:30,232 --> 00:04:31,512
But would that help?

82
00:04:32,132 --> 00:04:35,592
If a choice popped out of pure chance,
it wouldn't be yours either.

83
00:04:36,112 --> 00:04:39,172
It would be a dice roll
that happened to land in your head.

84
00:04:39,783 --> 00:04:41,083
So that's the trap.

85
00:04:41,703 --> 00:04:44,703
If your decision was caused,
you didn't control the cause.

86
00:04:45,403 --> 00:04:47,223
If it wasn't caused, it was random.

87
00:04:47,968 --> 00:04:51,488
Either way, it's hard to see
where free will is supposed to fit.

88
00:04:54,128 --> 00:04:58,428
Some philosophers reply that free
will was never about escaping causes.

89
00:04:59,328 --> 00:05:03,648
Being free means acting on your own
reasons, without anyone forcing you.

90
00:05:04,125 --> 00:05:07,125
By that definition,
you're free most of the time.

91
00:05:07,825 --> 00:05:09,605
But it's not the freedom most people mean

92
00:05:09,835 --> 00:05:13,625
when they ask whether free will exists:
the sense that, in exactly

93
00:05:13,855 --> 00:05:16,165
the same moment,
we could have done otherwise.

94
00:05:17,065 --> 00:05:18,265
There's a catch.

95
00:05:18,645 --> 00:05:20,985
You only ever live one version of events.

96
00:05:21,745 --> 00:05:25,245
You can't go back to that moment
and choose again to see what happens.

97
00:05:25,745 --> 00:05:27,125
So whether you could have done otherwise

98
00:05:27,355 --> 00:05:30,345
is something nobody can check,
not even you.

99
00:05:30,906 --> 00:05:33,566
Nobody has proved
that this feeling is an illusion.

100
00:05:33,946 --> 00:05:37,406
But suppose it is.
What would actually change?

101
00:05:40,366 --> 00:05:45,626
Start with punishment. Today,
we punish partly to protect other people,

102
00:05:45,706 --> 00:05:48,966
and partly because we feel
some people deserve to suffer.

103
00:05:49,552 --> 00:05:52,752
Without free will,
that second reason gets shaky.

104
00:05:53,772 --> 00:05:56,352
If nobody could ultimately
have chosen otherwise,

105
00:05:56,452 --> 00:05:59,312
nobody ultimately deserves
to suffer for it.

106
00:05:59,847 --> 00:06:01,807
But the first reason survives.

107
00:06:02,407 --> 00:06:05,667
We would still need to keep
dangerous people away from others.

108
00:06:06,324 --> 00:06:09,944
Think of how we treat someone
with a dangerous infectious disease.

109
00:06:10,704 --> 00:06:12,544
We might keep them in quarantine.

110
00:06:12,704 --> 00:06:15,304
But we don't say they deserve it.

111
00:06:15,811 --> 00:06:18,651
Prisons built on that idea
would look different.

112
00:06:19,191 --> 00:06:22,871
Less about revenge.
More about prevention and repair.

113
00:06:23,675 --> 00:06:27,375
A sentence wouldn't be measured
by how angry the crime makes us,

114
00:06:27,515 --> 00:06:32,395
but by how dangerous the person still is,
and what it would take to change that.

115
00:06:32,809 --> 00:06:34,429
That cuts both ways.

116
00:06:35,009 --> 00:06:38,029
Someone who's no longer a risk
could go home sooner.

117
00:06:38,469 --> 00:06:42,209
Someone who still is might stay longer
than a judge would set today.

118
00:06:42,893 --> 00:06:46,173
The question in court would shift
from "How much does he deserve?"

119
00:06:46,873 --> 00:06:49,113
to "What will stop this
from happening again?"

120
00:06:51,503 --> 00:06:53,963
What about guilt? It wouldn't disappear.

121
00:06:54,623 --> 00:06:56,963
You'd still know
that what you did hurt someone.

122
00:06:57,595 --> 00:07:01,695
You could still apologise, make up
for the damage, and try to do better.

123
00:07:02,275 --> 00:07:05,835
None of that needs a self
standing outside the chain of causes.

124
00:07:06,675 --> 00:07:08,955
Achievement wouldn't vanish either.

125
00:07:09,575 --> 00:07:13,155
If you trained for years to run
a marathon, the training really happened.

126
00:07:13,975 --> 00:07:17,515
Your discipline came from somewhere,
but you still did something hard.

127
00:07:18,145 --> 00:07:21,325
What would change is how we see
the people who didn't make it.

128
00:07:22,125 --> 00:07:23,705
If your talent, your health

129
00:07:23,935 --> 00:07:26,805
and the people who believed in you
all came from somewhere,

130
00:07:26,885 --> 00:07:29,005
so did someone else's lack of them.

131
00:07:29,529 --> 00:07:32,089
Success would still deserve admiration.

132
00:07:32,789 --> 00:07:35,769
Failure just wouldn't prove
that someone didn't try.

133
00:07:36,411 --> 00:07:38,431
And regret still has a job.

134
00:07:38,951 --> 00:07:41,551
It's how a brain learns
not to repeat a mistake.

135
00:07:42,354 --> 00:07:45,254
It just doesn't need the belief
that in that exact moment,

136
00:07:45,334 --> 00:07:48,074
with that exact brain,
you could have done otherwise.

137
00:07:50,924 --> 00:07:52,184
But there's a worry.

138
00:07:52,404 --> 00:07:56,724
If people stop believing in free will,
won't they stop behaving well?

139
00:07:57,315 --> 00:08:01,135
In 2008, two psychologists tested that.

140
00:08:01,375 --> 00:08:05,655
Some volunteers read a passage
arguing that free will is an illusion.

141
00:08:06,455 --> 00:08:09,235
Then everyone took a test
where cheating was easy.

142
00:08:09,895 --> 00:08:13,535
The people who'd read about free
will being an illusion cheated more.

143
00:08:14,315 --> 00:08:17,455
It became one of the best-known findings
on the subject.

144
00:08:18,185 --> 00:08:20,065
But when other labs
repeated the experiment

145
00:08:20,295 --> 00:08:24,405
with twenty times as many people,
the effect didn't show up.

146
00:08:24,959 --> 00:08:29,279
So there's no good evidence that losing
faith in free will makes people worse.

147
00:08:31,969 --> 00:08:33,689
So what would we really lose?

148
00:08:34,209 --> 00:08:35,709
Probably less than it seems.

149
00:08:37,479 --> 00:08:39,999
We would still plan. Still decide.

150
00:08:40,579 --> 00:08:42,839
Still love, argue, try and fail.

151
00:08:43,279 --> 00:08:45,379
Your brain would still weigh its options.

152
00:08:45,979 --> 00:08:48,679
It just wouldn't be doing it
from outside the universe.

153
00:08:49,221 --> 00:08:52,641
And it wouldn't mean you could sit back
and let fate do the work.

154
00:08:53,243 --> 00:08:56,103
Your thinking, your effort,
your decision to try again,

155
00:08:56,183 --> 00:08:57,763
those aren't outside the chain of causes.

156
00:08:57,993 --> 00:09:02,843
They're part of it, often the part
that decides what happens next.

157
00:09:05,443 --> 00:09:08,063
What might change
is how we look at other people.

158
00:09:08,703 --> 00:09:12,483
Instead of asking, "How could anyone
make such a terrible choice?",

159
00:09:12,703 --> 00:09:16,003
we might ask more often,
"What made that choice possible?"

160
00:09:16,762 --> 00:09:21,122
With the teacher, we asked that question,
because the cause showed up on a scan.

161
00:09:21,507 --> 00:09:25,207
If free will doesn't exist,
everyone deserves the same question.

162
00:09:25,487 --> 00:09:28,347
Their causes just don't show up on a scan.

163
00:09:30,547 --> 00:09:32,427
So here's a question for you.

164
00:09:32,987 --> 00:09:35,507
If you found out tomorrow
that free will doesn't exist,

165
00:09:35,587 --> 00:09:38,977
who would you judge differently?
Let me know in the comments.
